# Rank 0 encodes a picture prompt's items as the prefill reaches them. A request's pictures were all run through the
# tower inside its admission, on the scheduler thread (no stream decodes meanwhile: with full-size pictures a 50-picture
# first read is about 10x today's tower work), and their feature rows (8 KiB a row) stayed on the GPU for the stream's
# whole life, decode included, adding up across lanes against a reserve sized for one request. Now the feed holds the
# prompt's items (VisionFeed(prepared=...)); before each prompt chunk's FILL goes out, between rounds, it encodes the
# items whose rows that chunk covers (_feed_ready -> ensure), and the prefill lets go of the items it has passed
# (release), so a stream holds at most the rows of the items one chunk covers, and the lane drops its feed once its
# prompt is in. An encode that fails fails that stream alone, finished as a cancelled one is, before rank 1 hears of
# the chunk. A request's stats say how long its pictures took and how many were encoded (encode_s, pictures_encoded).
# A picture stream can now be handed back when the pool is full and replays with its pictures (it was failed instead).
# Picture rows must sit on the image token, checked at admission. mgllm, 2026-10-08.
# Tests: tests/test_9014_picture_feed.py (CPU: a fake tower; each chunk's rows equal encoding all items at once).

